              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "vanessajbaca"
            },
            "reviewBody": "If you use extra virgin olive oil in place of the vegetable oil, it will be spectacular...I promise. I love bagna calda with endive lettuce leaves (if you can find them) and red peppers, but when the calda gets cold and thick, it makes a fantastic spread for bread or tortillas."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "cynthia j"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Wow, yes olive oil makes it spectacular! Also, the sardines I had on hand were packed in hot sauce, not oil...added a nice kick to this.",
            "datePublished": "2008-04-04T12:30:44.163Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "NormalCook"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Use your best olive oil instead of vegetable oil. My father-in-law made this every Christmas Eve for 20 years in his electric skillet. Sometimes he'd add heavy creme to smooth it out. Serve with nice Italian bread, romaine lettuce and whole celery sticks. Dip your lettuce or celery and catch the drippings with your bread. So, so good! Makes the house smell like garlic for days!",
            "datePublished": "2016-07-24T21:49:32.940Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "1"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "GentlePecan8777"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've made bagna cauda every year for thanksgiving crudités and my guests are always amazed at how delicious it is. This recipe is too greasy and fishy. I begin with 2 cups heavy cream. Reduce it by boiling gently until only 1 cup remains. Then stir in 8 cloves finely minced garlic and 4-8 mashed anchovy filets that have been sautéed in sweet butter until smooth. Serve immediately over a spirit lamp to keep it warm. Bread and raw veggies are perfect dips.",
            "datePublished": "2025-03-10T20:27:31.000Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "LilacCod4575"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We've eaten this for generations, but now mostly just on New Year's Day with family. We eat is fondue - not a dip. (\"Bagna Caulda\" literally means \"hot bath\"). We use much more oil and a stick of butter no sardines, and cloves and cloves of garlic. Combine in an electric skillet that sits on the table, heat the oil and garlic up / melt diwn butter and sardines, and eat as a fondue. We mostly use cabbage, bread, and veggies, but we've started adding shrimp and cooked roast beef / chicken",
            "datePublished": "2025-03-10T17:43:21.285Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "maryann"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a family recipe from my grandfather who grew up in Asti. We use extra virgin olive oil and a stick of butter. No sardines, but anchovies packed in sea salt and well rinsed. Very hard to find, but well worth it. Try dipping thin strips of a very tender beef steak. Out of this world!",
            "datePublished": "2018-01-15T19:57:48.030Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "HeartySnap4311"
            },
            "reviewBody": "A sentimental favorite of our family. As other members have suggested, use olive oil and skip the sardines. We serve ours in a small sized crockpot to keep it nicely warm.",
            "datePublished": "2025-03-11T14:42:54.370Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
